  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 PM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nOverview: Minimal changes required from the previous forecast \npackage with overall dry conditions prevailing today and tomorrow,\nbefore the arrival of a tropical airmass on Monday. \n\nWeekend...Ridging will maintain control over the region this \nweekend, with generally rain-free conditions prevailing. The \nexception being the eastern periphery of Hudspeth County which could \nsee isolated convection later today due to a weak passing mid-level \ndisturbance combined with residual moisture (PWATs ~1.2\").\n\nMonday-Wednesday Morning...Rain and thunderstorm chances increase \nsignificantly (60-90%) early next week in response to a moisture \ninflux over the area as the remnants of Polo merge with an upper-\nlevel closed low passing over the Southwest. Models are \nindicating record PWATs (1.4-1.6\") areawide Monday night into \nTuesday which will make it for another very active period. Heavy \nrainfall and flooding remain the main threats with this next \npassing system, as soils are already saturated from this past week\nstorms. Heavy rainfall could begin as early as Monday afternoon \nlasting through Wednesday morning before clearing. Accumulations \nwill most likely range around 1-2\" across southern New Mexico and \nfar West Texas with localized amounts up to 3-4\". Although we \ncurrently are under a Slight Risk for Excessive Rainfall Monday \nand Tuesday, with the increased flood concern and higher \nconfidence in the forecast, anticipate these to be upgraded in \nlater packages. A Flash Flood Watch will also need to be evaluated\nat a later package. Stay tuned for the latest forecast updates. \n\nWednesday afternoon into next weekend...Rain chances are forecast\nto come to an end through the day on Wednesday as the aforementioned\nupper-level system drifts eastward and much drier air filters in \nbehind it as ridging builds back up. The rest of next week is \nshaping to be much quieter with plenty of sunshine and seasonable \ntemperatures. \n\nFlood Safety...Flash Flooding can escalate quickly. Highly urbanized \nareas, arroyos, and low-water crossings will face the highest risk \nof swift, dangerous water flows. Make sure to check the forecast \noften over the coming days for the latest updates and have at least \ntwo ways to received the Warnings. Never enter floodwaters, only 12\" \nof fast-moving water can carry away a car. Turn around, don't drown!",
